\begin_layout Part
How to read this book
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
From the piece to the instrument
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The thesis that goes with this book asks how a free reed makes its sound, and what the air around it does to that sound. This book asks a humbler question, the one a maker asks at the bench and a repairer asks with an instrument open in front of him or her: what does this piece do, why is it made the way it is, how do I know it is right, and what do I do when it is not?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An accordion is a box of pressure with doors in it. The bellows makes the pressure, the reeds turn a little of it into sound, the pallets decide which reeds get it, the keyboard opens the pallets, and every joint between two pieces of wood, card, leather or wax is a place where the air may leave without singing. Each part of this book takes one of these pieces and follows the same four steps. First, what physics says about it, kept to the few laws that really decide its behaviour. Then the analysis of a real instrument with those laws: which numbers matter, which do not. Then the tools for the maker, rules of thumb that come out of the laws, with the calculation behind them. And last, the repair bench: the symptoms a player brings, what they point to, and the gesture that checks it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The acoustics proper, the tongue, its chamber, the coupling of two voices and the sound radiated, belongs to the thesis and is only referred to here. What this book keeps is the physics of making: forces, flows, leaks, stiffness, friction, wood that moves.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two habits run through every part. The first is to say, each time, where a number comes from: measured, computed, taken from the workshop practice of Ewen and his process sheets, or proposed and still to be checked. Many figures in these pages are of the last kind, and they say so. The second is that every calculation can be redone with the true dimensions of the instrument on the bench. The small program outils_atelier.py (research/scripts) does all the arithmetic of this book, from the leak of a hole to the spring of a pallet, and its tests check that it agrees with the tables printed here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the instruments you want this book to speak about first, with their models (21/8, 33/12, 33/18, 33/24 of the training courses, the Lib RT, others), so that the examples use their true dimensions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Right-hand keyboard
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
A lever, a spring and a door
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take one key of the right hand out of an instrument and look at it. It is a lever turning on an axle shared with all the others. At one end, a button that the finger presses. At the other, a pallet, a small board faced with leather, lying on a hole of the soundboard. Between the two, a spring that keeps the pallet down when nobody plays. That is all. Three pieces and an axle, repeated thirty-three times on the instruments of the training courses, and almost everything a player feels under the right hand comes from the way these three pieces share one job: to keep a door shut against the wind, and to open it at once when asked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the workshop of Ewen the keys are cut on the numerical milling machine and the levers are bent and drilled by hand, the longest single task of the whole instrument (twelve hours for one right hand in the process sheets of 2026, an estimate). The trainee threads them on the axle in order, greases it, adds spacers where needed, sets every spring to 90 grams on a jig, glues the pallets with their jig and checks them against a lamp, then sets the buttons of a row at the same height with a rule. Each of these gestures has a reason in the physics, and this chapter gives it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The door against the wind
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A pallet closed on its hole carries the pressure of the bellows on its whole area: a force 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p\,A$
\end_inset
. On an accordion the pallet sits on the outer face of the soundboard, so the two directions of the bellows do opposite things. On the push, the air inside is above the atmosphere and it lifts the pallet: the spring must hold it down. On the draw, the outside air presses the pallet onto its seat: the finger pays that force when it opens the note. The chapter on the left-hand mechanism follows the second burden in detail; the right hand meets the same two, only smaller, because its holes are smaller.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How strong must the spring be? Strong enough that no pallet lifts on the strongest push. The workshop criterion, a proposal still to be calibrated on real instruments, is that no pallet lifts below 1.5 times the highest playing pressure. With a playing pressure of 3 kPa at most, the force at the pallet must be
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
F \ge 1.5\,p_{\max}\,A = 4500\,\mathrm{Pa}\times A
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which, written in the units of the bench, is a rule worth remembering: about half a gram at the pallet for every square millimetre of hole (0.46 g per mm² exactly, for 3 kPa). Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:rh-spring"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 gives it for a few holes.
\end_layout

