\begin_layout Standard
Which way does it push? It depends on which side of the board the door is. On an accordion the pallet sits on the outer face, the side of the atmosphere: on the push the air of the bellows lifts it, on the draw the outside air clamps it down. An organ puts its pallet on the other side, inside the wind chest, and the signs swap. There is no free side. Where the pressure helps to open, a spring must be strong enough to keep the door shut against the strongest push of the player; where the pressure resists, the finger pays it at the opening. The sum of the two burdens is the same either way. Only a door that is pushed equally in both senses escapes the bargain, and we shall meet it below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the Lib RT the burden falls on the spring. It must hold the pallet shut against the peak of the push, with a margin for the jolts of the bellows, and it must also press the leather hard enough to seal:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
F_{0} = 1.2 \, \Delta p_{\mathrm{peak}} \, A + F_{\mathrm{seal}}
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With a peak of 3 kPa and a sealing force of 0.3 N, this is 1.38 N (141 g) at the pallet. A weaker spring leaks at the forte push: the note speaks without being pressed, the old fault of accordions with tired springs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What does the finger feel while the door opens? Three things are added: the preload, the stiffness of the spring, and the pressure. The last one does not stay. As soon as the pallet lifts, the air runs through two orifices in series, the curtain between pallet and seat (perimeter times lift) and the tongue itself, and the pressure left on the pallet falls as 
\begin_inset Formula $1/(1+(P x / A_{\mathrm{reed}})^{2})$
\end_inset
. For a grave tongue with an effective section of about 20 mm², a quarter of the pressure force is left at 0.5 mm of lift, 8 % at 1 mm, almost nothing at 2 mm. Here is the step of the first paragraph: on the draw the finger first pays 1.38 + 0.60 = 1.98 N at the lift-off, then 1.50 N at one millimetre, then only the spring, 1.56 N at mid-stroke. Organ builders call it the pluck. On the push the pressure helps at the start (0.78 N) and the two directions meet again after a millimetre. The inequality between push and draw lives in the first millimetre and nowhere else.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The price of lift
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Why should the lift of the pallet matter to the finger? Because energy has nowhere to hide. If the button goes down by a travel 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset
 and the pallet goes up by a lift 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset
, then without losses 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{b} c = F_{s} x$
\end_inset
, and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
F_{b} = F_{s} \, \frac{x}{c} \, \frac{1}{\eta} = F_{s} \, \frac{r}{\eta}
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset
 the efficiency of the chain, about 0.9 with steel pivots. The ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset
 of lift to travel is not a detail of the drawing: it multiplies everything. A lift of 10 mm for a travel of 5 mm doubles the force at the button.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And how much lift does the air need? The air does not go through the pallet: it leaves sideways, through the thin slit that opens between the edge of the hole and the leather. That slit runs all the way round the hole, like a curtain hung from the pallet; its height is the lift 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset
, its length is the perimeter 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset
 of the hole, the length of its edge, so its area is 
\begin_inset Formula $P x$
\end_inset
 (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:lift"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
). As long as the curtain is smaller than the hole, it is the curtain that throttles the air, and lifting more helps. The pallet stops limiting the flow when the curtain equals the area 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 of the hole, at 
\begin_inset Formula $x = A/P$
\end_inset
; for a round hole of diameter 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset
 this is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $d/4$
\end_inset
. For 20 by 15 mm, 
\begin_inset Formula $A/P$
\end_inset
 = 300 / 70 = 4.3 mm; with a margin of 20 %, 5.1 mm. Above that, a pallet that opens further gives no more air, and the pressure on it has already vanished after the first millimetre. A centimetre of lift buys nothing for the sound and costs 60 to 100 % more at the finger. Whether the sound of the tongue is more open beyond 
\begin_inset Formula $A/P$
\end_inset
 is a question for the ear, and the bench can answer it (experiment E-hole below); nothing in the physics expects it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:button-force"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
