\begin_layout Standard
The second shape is the flat joint: two surfaces that should touch and do not quite, a plate on its wax, a reed block on its gasket, a frame on a frame, a register slide on its bed. Here the air does not jet; it creeps through a gap far thinner than it is long, and viscosity rules. Between two plane faces a distance 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset
 apart, over a length 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset
 of joint and across a land of width 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
, the flow follows the law of Poiseuille,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
q = \frac{w\,h^{3}\,\Delta p}{12\,\mu\,L}
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset
 the viscosity of air. Two features make this law the most useful one of the whole book. The flow is proportional to the pressure, not to its square root, so a joint and a hole can be told apart by measuring at two pressures: a leak that doubles when the pressure doubles is a joint; one that grows by only 1.4 times is a hole. And the flow grows as the cube of the gap. Halve the gap, divide the leak by eight. A joint is not a little worse when it is a little open; it is enormously worse.
\end_layout

