\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Finding a leak
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once a piece is known to leak, where? The gestures go from the cheapest to the cleverest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Smoke.
\series default
 A stick of incense moved slowly along the joints, piece under pressure: the smoke is blown away where the air leaves. Or piece under suction: the smoke is drawn in. 
\series bold
A tube to the ear
\series default
, a mechanic's stethoscope made from a hose, moved at 5 mm from the joints with the piece at 1500 Pa or more: the noise of a turbulent jet grows very fast with its speed, so tripling the pressure makes the hiss 14 to 19 dB louder.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Making a leak sing.
\series default
 A jet through a small hole is turbulent and hisses, with a noise that rises high, up to the ultrasound, where the voice of the reeds and the noise of the room are weak. The hiss is still lost in the room. The method proposed in this work makes it stand out by giving it a rhythm: the bellows modulates the pressure slowly, as a sine at a known frequency of 2 to 10 Hz, and every leak then hisses louder and softer at that frequency. A cheap microphone moved over the surface records; the program keeps the band of the hiss, about 4 to 20 kHz, takes its envelope, and keeps only what beats exactly at the frequency of the bellows, with the right phase. This is synchronous detection, the lock-in amplifier of the physics laboratory: everything that does not beat at our rhythm is rejected, and the leak appears on a map (banc_recherche/leak.py: lockin, acoustic_leak_strength, leak_map). Two fixed microphones can also locate a leak without moving: its hiss reaches them with a small delay that depends on its position, and the cross-correlation of the two signals gives that delay.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Making a leak visible.
\series default
 Two further methods are proposed and not built. Background-oriented schlieren films a printed speckled background through the jet: air of a different density bends the light a little, and a program comparing the images reveals the invisible jet, all the more if the air is warm and moist like a breath. Or the bellows is filled with carbon dioxide and the surface scanned with a small gas sensor of a few euros: the concentration rises at the leaks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Letting each reed sign its leak.
\series default
 A leak near a reed steals part of its air: the reed starts later, sounds softer, and goes out of tune in its own way. The tuner of this work already measures the deviation, the level and the response time of each reed; a local leak leaves its trace there, without any scanning microphone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): which of these you already use, and the two you would like to try first. And the area of the box of your gasometer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
At the repair bench
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Every note soft at the attack, the instrument tiring.
\series default
 A global leak. Count the drop test, push and draw; if it is under a minute, close the instrument piece by piece (bellows between boards, then each half-case) and find where the air goes before looking for the spot.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A ghost note at the fortissimo.
\series default
 Either a pallet that lifts (the right-hand part gives the force its spring needs) or a valve of the silent tongue that does not close. The bell on the suspect pallet answers the first; the second is found by the reed itself: a reed that sounds faintly in the wrong direction has a valve to replace.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
One note late and soft, the others fine.
\series default
 A leak near that reed: the wax around its plate, its valve, its chamber. Tape the slot of its tongue and test the block on the gasometer; flow that disappears when the plate is pressed down is a plate to rewax.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Wax and gaskets.
\series default
 A joint that leaks is not cured by more pressure on it but by a smaller gap: rewax the plate with a continuous bead, flatten the face of a block on abrasive paper laid on glass, replace a crushed gasket. The cube law says why a gap halved is a leak divided by eight.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the gestures you use to repair a leaking valve, a leaking pallet and a leaking plate, with their materials, so that this list is the one of your bench.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Bellows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
The lung of the instrument
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Everything upstream of the reed is in the bellows: the pressure, its rise, its fall, its reversal. A framed bellows is folds of card and cloth, leather at the corners, and a wooden frame at each end that takes the two halves of the case. On the instruments of the training courses it is bought ready made, and the trainee glues its frames, drills and fits the pins, and checks it alone before mounting it. This chapter asks three questions a maker meets before any of those gestures: what kind of source of air is a bellows, how large should it be, and where does it leak.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A source of flow, held by an arm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What does the arm actually set, the pressure or the flow? Every player has the answer in the hands. Keep the bellows moving at a steady speed and open a chord: the pressure drops, because the same flow is now shared by more reeds. A source of pressure, like the large weighted reservoir of an organ, would hold the pressure and send twice the flow through two reeds. A bellows behaves rather like a source of flow, and the truth lies between the two ideals: a real source gives less flow when the pressure rises. The simplest law is a straight line,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
q = q_0 - p/R
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_0$
\end_inset
 is the flow at zero pressure and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset
 the internal resistance of the source. The thesis shows what 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset
 does to the reed: it changes the amplitude of the note and moves its threshold. Here it matters for a simpler reason. A drone, a chord, a second voice, all draw on the same source, and the pressure each note receives depends on what the others are taking.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The arm itself is the other half of the source. A force 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset
 on a bellows of effective area 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset
 makes a pressure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
p = F/S
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and a stroke of length 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset
 sweeps a volume 
\begin_inset Formula $S\,c$
\end_inset
. The effective area is not the area of the frame: the folds give a little under pressure, and the force spreads over less than the full section. It is measured, not computed, by the drop test of the leak chapter: a known weight 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset
 on the bellows, the pressure read on the U-tube, and 
\begin_inset Formula $S = mg/p$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
How large a bellows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ewen answered the question of the size of the bellows in one sentence: a minimal section by number of voices; too small, not enough air; too large, not enough pressure; an optimum, perhaps to be computed. Here is the computation, and it is short.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Too large first. The arm has a force it can give comfortably for a phrase, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset
. To reach the pressure 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset
 that the reeds need for a forte, the area must not exceed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
S_{\max} = F/p.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Too small next. A phrase lasts a time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 between two reversals; the reeds that sound during it consume a flow 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset
, plus the leak; one stroke of length 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset
 must hold it all:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
S_{\min} = Q\,t/c.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Between the two lies the window, and Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:bellows-window"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 draws it with round numbers chosen only to make the arithmetic visible: an arm of 40 N, a stroke of 0.4 m, a phrase of 4 s, and a forte chord of nine reeds (three notes of three voices) at 10 L/min each. At 1000 Pa the window runs from 150 to 400 cm². At 2000 Pa it shrinks to 150 to 200 cm². Above about 2700 Pa it closes: a bellows large enough to hold the chord for four seconds can no longer be pushed to that pressure by that arm. The optimum that Ewen guessed is real, and it is the point where the two limits meet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename figures/pratique_soufflet_en.png
	width 100text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:bellows-window"

\end_inset

The window of the section of a bellows, with round example numbers to be replaced by measurements (outils_atelier.py soufflet). Above 
\begin_inset Formula $F/p$
\end_inset
 the arm no longer makes the pressure; below 
\begin_inset Formula $Qt/c$
\end_inset
 the air runs out before the end of the phrase. For a pianissimo of two reeds (dashed line) the air limit is negligible; it is the forte that closes the window.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two lessons hold whatever the true numbers. At the pianissimo, two reeds at 2 L/min ask for a section of 7 cm² only: the air side never binds, and the section is set by the forte. And the number of voices enters the minimum directly, which is Ewen's rule in algebra: a third voice is a third more air for the same phrase, and a bellows sized for two voices runs out sooner with three. But none of the inputs of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:bellows-window"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 has been measured. The flow of a reed at forte is the least known: the network models of the thesis give 6 to 24 L/min per tongue at 1000 Pa and are known to be too generous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the five numbers of the window, on one of your instruments. The force of your arm for a held forte, with a luggage scale hooked to the strap; the useful stroke of the bellows; the length of a typical phrase between two reversals; the flow of one reed at mezzo-forte and forte, by the descent of the gasometer with the tape removed; and the effective area of the bellows by the drop test. Then outils_atelier.py soufflet draws the true window.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Where a bellows leaks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The workshop knows where: at the corners and at the frames. The physics adds why the frames are so demanding. A frame is a flat joint, and a long one: about a metre around for a small instrument. By the cube law of the leak chapter, a frame joint 1000 mm long with 10 mm of land and a gap of 0.05 mm, the thickness of a hair, leaks 1.7 L/min at 500 Pa on its own, more than the whole budget of the bellows (under 1 L/min, Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:leak-budget"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
). At 0.02 mm it leaks 0.11 L/min; at 0.1 mm, 14 L/min. No pair of wooden frames is flat to two hundredths of a millimetre over a metre after a season of humidity, and that is why the frame needs its gasket: the gasket does not close the gap, it fills it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The corners are the other weak point: leather folded and glued at the place where the card bends most, and where it wears first. A hole there is a hole, not a joint, and Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:leak-hole"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 gives its weight: one millimetre is almost a litre per minute.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Making and checking the bellows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Frames glued, holes drilled, pins fitted.
\series default
 The bellows must go onto the case without forcing and with no light through. A frame that has to be forced will spring back and open its joint; one with light through it is already a leak of the size of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:joint"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The bellows alone, between two boards.
\series default
 Close it between two boards with gaskets, hang a known weight, film the descent with a ruler beside it, and pass incense smoke along the corners. The proposed figure is under 1 L/min at 500 Pa. The same measurement gives the effective area of the bellows, 
\begin_inset Formula $mg/p$
\end_inset
, which the window above needs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the bellows is bought, with a lead time of two months. What do you check on arrival, and what makes you send one back?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
At the repair bench
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The instrument tires the player, the drop test is short.
\series default
 Close the bellows alone between two boards: if the leak stays, it is in the bellows; if it goes, it is in the cases or the frames. Then the smoke, corner by corner.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Pin-holes in the folds.
\series default
 A small lamp put inside the bellows in a dark room shows every pin-hole as a point of light; a worn corner shows as a glow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Leak at a frame.
\series default
 Look at the gasket first: crushed, hardened, or torn, it no longer fills the gap, and the cube law says that a few hundredths of a millimetre are enough to lose the budget. Replace it before touching the wood.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A bellows that has become soft, or stiff.
\series default
 Card that has lost its spring, or glue that has gone hard at the folds. It changes the feel more than the air, and its measure is the effective area: compare 
\begin_inset Formula $mg/p$
\end_inset
 with that of a bellows the player likes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the repairs of a bellows that you do yourself and those you send away, and how long each takes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Reed blocks and plates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
A wooden comb under the reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A reed block is a wooden comb. Each tooth is a wall, and between two walls lies a chamber; a reed plate with its two tongues closes the chamber on one side, the soundboard with its hole on the other. On the instruments of the training courses the blocks are milled two by two from one blank, with their heels, and deburred before the course; the trainee checks that they fall in front of the holes of the soundboard, counter-drills the fixings, glues each block on its heels, waxes the plates into their places, checks the furnished block for leaks, and screws it onto the soundboard with M3 screws into threaded inserts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What the shape of the chamber does to the sound, the volume of its cells, the hole, the coupling of the two voices of a note, is acoustics, and the thesis treats it. This chapter keeps what decides whether a block is right in the hands of the maker: that it is tight, that every reed has its room, and that what is screwed can be taken apart without changing the note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Flat, or not tight
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every face of a block is a flat joint: plate on wax, block on heel, block on soundboard. The cube law of the leak chapter applies to all of them, and it is severe. A joint of 100 mm around with 5 mm of wood to cross leaks 0.05 L/min at 500 Pa, the proposed figure for a whole block, with a gap of only 0.026 mm (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:joint"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
). A block has several such joints, so each must do better. No milling machine and no hand plane leave two faces that close; something must fill the gap. That is the job of the wax under and around a plate, of the glue between block and heel, and of the gasket or the mastic between block and soundboard. Seen from the cube law, the choice between them is a choice of how a gap is filled, and of whether it can be filled again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A glued block is tight by nature and does not come off. A screwed block comes off for tuning and repair, and then everything rests on its gasket: crushed once too often, the gasket no longer fills the gap, and the leak comes back with the cube of the lost hundredths. Wax around a plate seals and can be redone with a spoon; it softens with heat and hardens with age. Nails through a strip of leather hold the plate mechanically and leave the sealing to the leather. What these choices do to the tongue itself, its damping and its pitch, is a question of the thesis (the chapter on reed coupling), and it has no answer yet beyond the protocol that would give one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A leak between two chambers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a leak that the gasometer does not see, because it does not go outside. If the face of a block does not seal against the soundboard between two chambers, air goes from one chamber to its neighbour. When the pallet of one note opens, a little air reaches the next chamber, whose pallet is shut; if a reed there is close to its threshold, it sounds faintly. A ghost note on a neighbour, not on the note itself, points to this leak under the block rather than to a pallet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Each reed in its room
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The booklet asks that no reed touch the bottom of its cell or the partition. The reason is plain: a tongue that grazes wood in its swing does not speak, or buzzes. The milling program checks the room of each reed on the model before cutting (a reed laid in its case does not touch the partition); the trainee checks it again after waxing, because a bead of wax can push a plate off its line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the clearance you leave between a reed and its partition and bottom, and whether it changes from the treble to the bass, where the tongues swing wider.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Making and checking the blocks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each step of the course, with its reason.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Sort the reeds by block, then by row, then by note,
\series default
 each in front of its case marked A or B. A plate in the wrong case is a note in the wrong place, and once waxed it is a repair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Wax each plate,
\series default
 the wax warmed in a bottle warmer and poured with a spoon all round the plate, the bead continuous. The bead is the filler of the cube law: a gap in it is a hole, and a hole of one millimetre is almost a litre per minute.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Check the furnished block,
\series default
 its slots closed with tape, on the gasometer, in pressure then in suction, because a plate can be pressed onto its wax in one direction and pulled away from it in the other. Smoke to find a leak. Proposed figure: under 0.05 L/min at 500 Pa.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Measure the flow of each reed,
\series default
 tape removed one at a time, by the descent of the gasometer at 100, 300 and 500 Pa. This is not an examination but a measurement: the air that reed consumes, which the bellows chapter needs and which nobody has yet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Screw the blocks onto the soundboard,
\series default
 M3 screws into the inserts, then a lamp: no light between block and soundboard.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the gasket or mastic between block and soundboard on your instruments, and how many times a screwed block can be taken off before its gasket must be changed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
At the repair bench
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
One note late and soft.
\series default
 Tape its slot and test the block: if the leak goes when you press the plate, rewax it; if it stays, look at the chamber and its face on the soundboard.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A neighbour that whispers.
\series default
 A leak between two chambers under the block. Take the block off, look at the face against the soundboard and at the gasket; flatten the face on abrasive paper laid on glass if it is no longer plane.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A note that changed after the blocks were taken off and put back.
\series default
 The thesis asks whether remounting a block moves the pitch of its tongues by more than the few cents that the chamber itself moves them; the bench is where the answer will come from. Note the deviation of each note with the tuner before and after.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A reed that buzzes.
\series default
 It touches its cell, or its plate has moved in the wax. Look along the slot against a light, and rewax straight.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): when a screwed block is taken off and put back, does any tongue change pitch by more than a few cents? Three notes, with the tuner, before and after.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Reeds at the bench
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
A spring that sings
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A reed is a tongue of spring steel riveted at its heel onto a plate, over a slot a few hundredths of a millimetre wider than itself. On the other face of the plate, a second tongue lies over a second slot, the other way round, and each slot has a valve, a flap of leather or plastic, on the side from which its air does not come. The thesis spends a whole part on what makes such a tongue sound. This chapter asks what the maker and the repairer do with it: how to tune it, how to set it, what to measure, and what to do when it will not speak.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Where to scrape
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A tongue is a spring with a mass, and its frequency goes as the square root of stiffness over mass. A file or a scraper can take away either, depending on where it bites. Near the tip, the steel moves the most and bends the least: scraping there removes mass where it counts and stiffness where it does not, and the note rises. Near the heel, the steel bends the most and moves the least: scraping there removes stiffness, and the note falls. Hence the rule printed on the strobe of the tuner of this work: too high, scrape towards the heel; too low, scrape towards the tip.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How sensitive is it? For a blade of even thickness, the frequency is proportional to the thickness: thin the whole tongue by one per cent and the note falls by one per cent, 17 cents, a sixth of a semitone. A local scrape does far less than an even thinning, which is precisely why scraping can be done at all, a stroke at a time with the tuner watching. The same proportion explains why handbook values of the steel will not do for tuning: the stiffness of spring steel varies by about 3 per cent with its grade, which is 26 cents on the note. The thesis gives the way to measure it on a strip of the same steel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
At which pressure to tune
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every version of the models of the thesis says that the note of a free reed goes down when the player pushes harder. A reed tuned at one pressure is therefore slightly out of tune at the others, and the question "at which pressure do you tune?" is not a detail. On Ewen's own instrument the tuner found differences of 15 to 25 cents between push and draw on the same notes, which is the other face of the same question: the two tongues of a plate are two different blades, tuned separately, and they must agree at the pressure the music uses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): at which pressure, or with which bellows gesture, you tune; whether it is the same for bass and treble; and your tolerance in cents for a training-course instrument.
\end_layout

