The thesis adds a warning that the tuner should know. Two tongues that share the air of one pallet are coupled, and the network model predicts that close to the unison they pull each other into step: below about 2 cents, at A4 and 1000 Pa, the two voices lock onto one frequency and the beat disappears; at 5 cents the beat is still 12 per cent slower than the difference of the blades; at 20 cents it is free. Near the lock the beat heard is not the difference of the blades but 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{\Delta^2-\Delta_L^2}$
\end_inset
, the law of two coupled oscillators. If the model is right, a voice tuned at 2 cents of its partner will not beat at all once both are free, and a slow tremolo must be tuned a little wider than its target. Experiment E3 of the thesis tests it with the tuner: tune one tongue with the other blocked by a strip of paper, free it, and read whether the beat survives.
