\begin_layout Section
The set of the tongue, and the four pressures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tip of a tongue does not lie flat in its slot; it stands a little above it, the set or lift. The lift decides how the reed starts and how far it can be pushed. Four pressures describe it, all measurable with the bench: the pressure at which it starts, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
, the softest pianissimo one can attack; the pressure at which it chokes and falls silent although the pressure is still rising, the loudest fortissimo; and, coming back down, the pressure at which it speaks again and the one at which it goes out, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{off}}$
\end_inset
, the softest pianissimo one can hold. Ewen's aim for a block, the widest range of play with the lowest threshold, is written in these four numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The working hypothesis, from the classic work of St Hilaire and colleagues extended in this project, is that at equal geometry the starting pressure grows as the square of frequency times lift, while the choking pressure grows as stiffness times lift over area. A low lift would then start easily and choke early; a high lift would hold a fortissimo and refuse a pianissimo. Experiment E19 measures the four pressures for three reeds at three lifts, and would contradict the hypothesis if the starting pressure did not move with the lift.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): how you set the lift today (by eye, by gauge), and the reeds you would choose for E19, one bass, one middle, one treble.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The valve
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The valve makes the flow through a slot one-way. Without it, the air of the bellows would leak through the slot of the silent tongue of each plate, and that tongue, if close to its threshold, would whisper: a ghost note. The models of the thesis take the valve as perfect; a real one has a mass, leaks a little, and slaps against the plate at each reversal of the bellows, which is where the tuner of this work sees the reversal. The thesis has a protocol for comparing leather and plastic valves; at the bench, the valve is a part that wears, curls, sticks or comes unglued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): your valves are put on before the course or by the trainee? With which glue, and how?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Tools at the bench
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The tuner of this work
\series default
 shows each reed at once in the manner of a strobe and says what to do with the tongue; four lights come on as the reed approaches its target, at 5, 1, 0.3 and 0.1 cent. The register mode measures two to five voices together without blocking any, shows the beat of each detuned voice, and a list of beats, the target tremolo, can be copied from one instrument to another. Push and draw are measured and kept apart.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The U-tube
\series default
 gives the pressure at which a reed is tuned or its thresholds read; 
\series bold
the gasometer
\series default
 gives the air a reed consumes; 
\series bold
feeler gauges and a lamp
\series default
 give the lift and the gaps; 
\series bold
a phone filming at 240 frames per second
\series default
 shows a bass tongue swing, and whether its tip leaves the slot.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A plucked tongue
\series default
 says a great deal before any air: pluck it with a fingernail, record it with a phone, and the frequency and the damping come out of the recording (pince.py in the thesis). A tongue that rings much shorter than its neighbours has a loose rivet, a crack, or something touching it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
At the repair bench
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A reed that does not speak.
\series default
 In order of likelihood: it touches the slot or the cell (look against a light), its lift is lost (the tip lies in the slot), the valve of its partner is missing so its air leaks away, the wax around its plate leaks, or dirt sits in the slot. A thin feeler passed round the tongue finds a grain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A reed that speaks late or only loud.
\series default
 Its threshold is high: lift too great, or a leak that takes its pressure (the leak part of this book). Compare its starting pressure with a neighbour on the U-tube.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A buzz or a rattle.
\series default
 A tongue grazing, a rivet loose (the pluck rings short), a valve slapping or half unglued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A note out of tune after years.
\series default
 Retune it, but look first: rust adds mass where it forms, and a pitted tongue may crack later. A cracked or broken tongue is replaced, not tuned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): your list of reed repairs, from the most frequent, and the supplier delay for a replacement reed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Registers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
A door for a whole row of reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A register closes or opens a whole set of reeds at once: a slide pierced with a row of holes moves under the reed blocks, and its holes either face the holes of the chambers or not. It is a pallet for a row instead of a note, and it shares the troubles of both pallets and joints. This chapter is short, because nothing has been measured on a register in this work; it says what the physics of the previous parts predicts, so that the first measurements know what to look for.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Tight and free at once
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A slide must do two things that pull against each other. It must seal, so that a closed register stays silent; and it must move, so that the player can change registers in a breath. Sealing asks for a small gap, and the cube law of the leak part is merciless: a slide of 200 mm with a land of 5 mm on each side of its holes, a gap of 0.05 mm, leaks of the order of a litre per minute at 500 Pa. Moving asks for little friction, and friction grows with the force that presses the slide onto its bed: 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{\mathrm{move}} = \mu N$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The cube law also says where the compromise lies. Because the leak goes as the cube of the gap, a modest reduction of the gap buys a large reduction of the leak: a gap smaller by a fifth halves the leak. A slide does not need to be crushed onto its bed to seal; it needs a bed that is flat and a light, even pressure, from a spring or a felt, that keeps it there.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Push and draw are not the same
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The slide sits between the air of the bellows and the chambers, and the bellows pushes on it in one direction and pulls on it in the other, exactly as it does on a pallet. On one side of the bellows the pressure presses the slide onto its bed and helps it seal; on the other it lifts it and opens the gap. A register can therefore be tight on the push and whisper on the draw, and must be tested in both directions, like a furnished reed block.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A closed register that whispers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A closed register that leaks feeds the chambers of its row. Their reeds receive a little air, and those close to their threshold sound faintly behind the others: a ghost voice rather than a ghost note, heard most on the soft notes of the row.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): which of your instruments have registers, how their slides are pressed onto their beds (spring, felt, leather), and the force needed to move one, read with a luggage scale or a gram gauge on the knob.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Making and checking
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Check a register the way a block is checked: the case closed, the register shut, the slots of its reeds open, on the gasometer, in pressure and in suction. The flow that disappears when the register is opened and its pallets kept shut is the leak of the slide. The proposed figure for the whole closed case, slides included, is under 0.5 L/min at 500 Pa.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
At the repair bench
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A register that is hard to move.
\series default
 Swollen wood or a hardened felt: the friction has grown. Look at the season first; a slide that binds in a damp winter may be free in summer, and planing it then makes it leak in the next dry spell.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A closed register that can be heard.
\series default
 A leak of the slide: a bed no longer flat, a felt crushed. The cube law again: restore the flatness and the even pressure before adding force.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the register troubles you meet most, and how you repair them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Case, grille and straps
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
The box that holds it all
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The case carries everything: the soundboard, the blocks, the mechanism, the bellows frame, the straps, and the hands. On the instruments of the training courses its sides are milled before the course and glued by the trainee in a gluing jig, dry-assembled first; the button board, its cap and the stiffeners follow, then levelling and sanding, varnish or oil. What the box and the grille do to the sound, a filter for the grille, a cavity for a resonance box, is acoustics and belongs to the thesis (chapter on timbral elements). This chapter keeps what decides whether the box is right for the maker: that it is tight, that it stays true through the seasons, and that the hands are well held.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Wood moves, joints open
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Wood is not a stable material, and the accordion is a box of joints. Between green and dry, wood shrinks of the order of 5 to 10 per cent across the grain and of the order of 0.1 to 0.2 per cent along it (Wood Handbook of the Forest Products Laboratory, orders of magnitude): across the grain it moves a few tens of times more than along it. A case is glued from pieces whose grains cross, so every change of humidity makes some joints work. A joint that worked last winter is a gap this summer, and the cube law of the leak part says what a gap of a few hundredths of a millimetre costs. This is the physical reason behind the advice of the booklet: keep the instrument in a dry place at a steady temperature, away from the damp cellar and the attic, and avoid sudden changes, a hot car then a cold room.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is also the reason for two habits of the workshop: no filler on a gluing surface, so that the glue holds wood to wood; and stiffeners glued flat with no light under them, so that the face they hold stays plane.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the species you use for the cases (the process sheets and the website do not agree, maple or lime), and the drying times of stiffeners, hard base and oil.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The grille
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The grille is the perforated screen in front of the right-hand pallets. For the maker it has one rule: it must touch neither the mechanism nor a button pressed down, otherwise it rubs, clicks, or holds a key down. Check it by pressing every button with the grille in place. For the sound, the grille is a filter whose cut-off depends on its open area and its depth; the thesis says how to measure it, by recording one note with and without the grille.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Straps
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The straps hold the instrument; the left-hand strap holds the hand against the bellows. On the training courses they are fixed on a support milled in the right-hand case and adjusted to the player, sitting and then standing. The booklet's check is the right one, and it is ergonomic, not acoustic: the instrument is stable sitting and standing, and the left thumb touches no screw.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): straps bought or made at the workshop? And the adjustment of the left-hand strap: how much play does the hand need to reverse the bellows freely?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Making and checking
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Case glued dry first.
\series default
 Assemble in the jig without glue; anything that does not close dry will not close glued. Then glue the four sides, check square and flat.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Closed case, leak checked.
\series default
 Close the case with a board in place of the bellows, on the gasometer, and pass the smoke along the joints, the frame, the mechanism and the register knobs. Proposed figure: under 0.5 L/min at 500 Pa.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
At the repair bench
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A case that leaks after a change of season.
\series default
 A joint that has opened. Smoke finds it; a seam of glue or, on a frame, a new gasket closes it. Plane only when the wood has settled to the humidity it will live in.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A grille that clicks or holds a button.
\series default
 It touches. Look for the shine of a rub on its inner face.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the case repairs you meet most, and how you do them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
The repairer's diagnosis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
From the symptom to the piece
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An instrument arrives at the bench with a sentence, not with a diagnosis: "it's tiring", "this note doesn't come", "there's a sound when I push hard". Each part of this book ended with the repair of its own piece. This last part goes the other way round, from what the player says to the piece that says it, so that the first quarter of an hour with an open instrument is spent checking, not guessing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Listen, then count
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Three gestures come before any screwdriver, and none takes more than a few minutes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Play it with the player.
\series default
 Ask which notes, which direction, which nuance. Most faults of an accordion depend on the direction of the bellows and on the pressure, because pallets, valves, slides and joints all see the bellows push in one direction and pull in the other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Count the drop test.
\series default
 A known weight on the bellows, air valve shut, no key: the time of the full drop, push and draw. Under 20 to 30 seconds, there is a frank leak and nothing else should be judged before it is found (the leak part, Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:drop"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Pass the tuner over the keyboard.
\series default
 Deviation, push and draw, and beat of each voice. A single note off among tuned ones points to that reed or to its air; a whole region off points to pressure, leaks or a block.
\end_layout

